  <h2>Yield definitions: how to read each number</h2>
  <p>When you see a "yield" on a financial site for an ETF, it can mean any of four different things. Knowing which one you're looking at changes your interpretation entirely.</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Yield type</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Best for</th><th>Example (ULTY, Aug 2026)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Trailing 12-month yield</strong></td>
        <td>Sum of last 12 months of distributions ÷ current price</td>
        <td>Comparing funds on a single common denominator</td>
        <td>~103–106%</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Distribution rate (issuer-reported)</strong></td>
        <td>Most recent distribution annualized ÷ NAV</td>
        <td>Forward-looking "if today's payout continues"</td>
        <td>~60% (YieldMax official)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>SEC 30-day yield</strong></td>
        <td>Net investment income (excludes option premiums, capital gains) over the last 30 days, annualized</td>
        <td>Comparing actual income generation</td>
        <td>−0.78% (negative)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Realized income yield (manual)</strong></td>
        <td>Distribution components that are <em>not</em> return of capital</td>
        <td>After-tax cash income for an investor</td>
        <td>~2% (recent monthly payouts are largely ROC)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The four numbers tell four different stories. A trailing yield of 100% is not "you earned 100% in a year" — it means the last 12 months of payouts, divided by today's NAV. A distribution rate of 60% is not "you will earn 60% next year" — it's a snapshot annualized from the latest distribution. An SEC 30-day yield of −0.78% tells you the fund's underlying portfolio is generating <em>less</em> income than its expense ratio. The realized income yield is the figure that matters for your tax bill, and for many option-income funds it is a small fraction of the headline number.</p>

  <h2>Comparison: same yield, very different fund</h2>
  <p>The table below shows four popular ETFs with distribution rates that look similar but reflect four different income engines.</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fund</th><th>Distribution rate</th><th>SEC 30-day yield</th><th>Income source</th><th>Tax treatment of distributions</th><th>Total return 1Y</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="/articles/schd-dividend-yield/">SCHD</a></td><td>~3.5%</td><td>~3.5%</td><td>Underlying company dividends (domestic)</td><td>Mostly qualified, 0–20%</td><td>Modestly positive</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/articles/jepi-dividend/">JEPI</a></td><td>~7.6%</td><td>~7.6%</td><td>Equity-linked notes + ELN option premiums</td><td>Mostly ordinary income (ELN)</td><td>Modestly positive, capped upside</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/articles/spyi-dividend-history/">SPYI</a></td><td>~12%</td><td>~0.5%</td><td>SPX index option premiums</td><td>Section 1256 60/40 + ROC</td><td>Tracks index, capped upside</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/articles/ulty-dividend/">ULTY</a></td><td>~60%</td><td>−0.78%</td><td>Multi-asset call spreads, heavy ROC</td><td>Mostly ROC (cost basis reduction)</td><td>~0% since launch</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Read across the row, not down the column. SCHD's 3.5% is real dividend income from US companies. ULTY's 60% is mostly the fund paying you back your own money — the headline number is real, but the cash flow is the same cash you put in.</p>

  <h2>Risk points specific to this category</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Return of capital hides NAV erosion.</strong> A 12% distribution that is 90% ROC means the fund's NAV drops by roughly 11% per year just from paying you — any price appreciation must overcome that drag. Use the issuer's 19a-1 notices, not the headline yield, to see the ROC share.</li>
    <li><strong>Distribution rate ≠ forward yield.</strong> When a covered-call fund's underlying volatility drops, the run-rate usually follows. YieldMax warned in late 2025 that future distributions "may be somewhat lower and less predictable" after strategy changes; ULTY's weekly payout fell from $0.47–$0.52 in January to $0.31 by August 2026.</li>
    <li><strong>Capped upside on covered-call funds.</strong> Funds that sell index options give up part of every strong rally in exchange for the monthly premium. Over a decade of flat or modest markets they can outperform; over a strong bull market they trail badly.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-stock option ETFs concentrate risk.</strong> MSTY/TSLY/CONY/QDTE are not diversified dividend ETFs. The covered call caps the upside of a single volatile name, but if that name falls 60% the strategy still owns most of the loss. Total return since launch for several of these is sharply negative.</li>
    <li><strong>Tax treatment varies.</strong> SCHD distributions are mostly qualified dividends. SPYI's index option premiums are Section 1256 (60/40 long-term/short-term split). MSTY/ULTY distributions are mostly ROC, not taxed now but reduce your cost basis.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Methodology and sources</h2>
  <p>Distribution amounts, ex-dates and payment dates come directly from each fund's official fund page and SEC Form N-CSR / 19a-1 distribution notices. Total return figures use split-adjusted series from the issuer and major data providers (StockAnalysis, Robinhood, Morningstar) and are cross-checked against the issuer's own monthly fact sheet. We update each article's <code>dataAsOf</code> whenever we touch the figures, and the most recent refresh is shown in the article footer's "Data through" line.</p>
  <p>For covered-call funds, the SEC 30-day yield is the most honest single number for actual income generation, but it understates total cash flow because it excludes option premiums. We therefore report both: SEC yield (for comparability) and distribution rate (for cash-flow planning), and we flag ROC percentage when the issuer discloses it.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <details class="faq-item" open><summary>Which dividend ETF has the longest history?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Among the funds we cover, SCHD has the longest continuous distribution record (since 2011), with VOO and traditional large-cap dividend ETFs going back further. For option-income ETFs, JEPI is the oldest (since 2020); SPYI dates to 2022; the YieldMax and NEOS families mostly began in 2024–2025.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Why do two ETFs both show 12% yield but one has flat total return?</summary><div class="faq-answer">One fund's 12% comes mostly from selling index options (SPYI); the other's comes mostly from return of capital (a heavy-ROC single-stock fund). The first can still track the underlying index over time; the second shrinks its own NAV by the payout amount. Always check the SEC 30-day yield and the issuer's ROC disclosure to know which kind of 12% you're looking at.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>How often are distributions paid?</summary><div class="faq-answer">It depends on the family. Traditional dividend-growth ETFs (SCHD, VOO) pay quarterly. NEOS option-income ETFs (QQQI, SPYI, IWMI) pay monthly. JPMorgan JEPI/JEPQ pay monthly. YieldMax single-stock ETFs (ULTY, MSTY, TSLY) pay weekly (Group 1 and Group 2 funds) or monthly. ProShares BITO pays monthly from realized futures gains.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Are dividend ETF distributions the same as stock dividends?</summary><div class="faq-answer">No. A stock dividend is cash paid by a company from its earnings. An ETF distribution is whatever the fund has received from its holdings (dividends, option premiums, realized gains) minus expenses, classified by tax character. For an equity ETF the dominant source is still dividends, but for an option-income ETF the distribution is mostly premium and ROC.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Where can I see the ROC percentage?</summary><div class="faq-answer">For US mutual funds and ETFs, the issuer files a Section 19(a) notice after every distribution showing the estimated split between ordinary income, capital gains, and return of capital. NEOS publishes monthly breakdowns on each fund page; YieldMax publishes 19a-1 notices on its Group 1 supplemental tax page; ProShares lists per-distribution tax character on the BITO distributions page.</div></details>

